> Carefully review the code before committing. Ensure that it accurately replaces the highlighted code, contains no missing lines, and has no issues with indentation. Thoroughly test & benchmark the code to ensure it meets the requirements.

```suggestion
	public static function get_pricing( string $model_id ): ?array {
		if ( isset( self::PRICING[ $model_id ] ) ) {
			return self::PRICING[ $model_id ];
		}

		$pricing_map = self::PRICING;
		uksort(
			$pricing_map,
			static fn( string $a, string $b ): int => strlen( $b ) <=> strlen( $a )
		);

		foreach ( $pricing_map as $key => $pricing ) {
			if ( str_starts_with( $model_id, $key ) ) {
				return $pricing;
			}
		}

		return null;
	}
```

</details>

<!-- suggestion_end -->

<details>
<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>

```
Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.

In `@includes/Core/CostCalculator.php` around lines 26 - 32, get_pricing()
currently picks the first prefix match which causes broader keys (e.g.,
'gpt-4o', 'gpt-4.1') to match before more specific keys like 'gpt-4o-mini' or
'gpt-4.1-nano', overstating costs; fix by changing the matching logic to prefer
the most specific keys first—e.g., sort the pricing map keys by descending
length (or specificity) before checking startsWith, so identifiers like
'gpt-4o-mini-*' or 'gpt-4.1-nano-*' will match 'gpt-4o-mini' / 'gpt-4.1-nano'
before 'gpt-4o' / 'gpt-4.1' in get_pricing().
```

</details>

