_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_

**Don’t silently drop JSON parse failures.**

Setting `body = null` on parse failure hides the actual backend response and makes test failures harder to diagnose. Return parse metadata (or raw text) and assert parse success before shape checks.



<details>
<summary>🔎 Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
- let body = null;
- try {
- 	body = await res.json();
- } catch ( e ) {
- 	body = null;
- }
- return { status: res.status, body };
+ const raw = await res.text();
+ let body = null;
+ let parseError = null;
+ try {
+ 	body = raw ? JSON.parse( raw ) : null;
+ } catch ( e ) {
+ 	parseError = String( e );
+ }
+ return { status: res.status, body, parseError, raw };
```

```diff
+ expect( apiResponse.parseError ).toBeNull();
+ expect( apiResponse.body ).not.toBeNull();
```
</details>


Also applies to: 138-143

<details>
<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>

```
Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.

In `@tests/e2e/changes-page.spec.js` around lines 102 - 107, The test currently
swallows JSON parse failures by setting body = null in the catch around await
res.json(), which hides the backend response; change the catch to capture and
store the raw response and the parse error (e.g., set body = { raw: await
res.text(), parseError: e }) instead of null, then assert parse success (or
explicitly fail) before performing shape checks on body; apply the same change
to the other identical try/catch around res.json() later in the file so tests
report the actual response and parsing error when JSON.parse fails.
```

</details>

