_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_

**Immediate `/job` completion makes the stop-button test timing-fragile.**

At **Line 80**, the first poll always returns `status: 'complete'`, which can clear sending state before the assertion in the stop-button test (Line 142-156) observes it. Return `'processing'` for at least one poll (or add a small completion delay) for that scenario.



<details>
<summary>Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
 async function interceptStream( page, options = {} ) {
-	const { generatedTitle } = options;
+	const { generatedTitle, processingPolls = 0 } = options;
+	let jobPollCount = 0;
@@
 	await page.route( /gratis-ai-agent\/v1\/job\//, async ( route ) => {
+		jobPollCount += 1;
+		if ( jobPollCount <= processingPolls ) {
+			await route.fulfill( {
+				status: 200,
+				contentType: 'application/json',
+				body: JSON.stringify( { status: 'processing' } ),
+			} );
+			return;
+		}
+
 		const result = {
 			status: 'complete',
 			session_id: capturedSessionId,
 			reply: 'Hello from the AI!',
 			...( generatedTitle ? { generated_title: generatedTitle } : {} ),
 		};
```

```diff
-		await interceptStream( page );
+		await interceptStream( page, { processingPolls: 1 } );
```
</details>


Also applies to: 86-90

<details>
<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>

```
Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.

In `@tests/e2e/chat-interactions.spec.js` around lines 74 - 81, The route handler
registered via page.route(/gratis-ai-agent\/v1\/job\//) returns status:
'complete' immediately which makes the stop-button test timing-fragile; change
the handler to return 'processing' for the first poll (or until a short delay)
and only return 'complete' on subsequent polls for the same session_id (use a
closure-scoped map or flag keyed by capturedSessionId to track first vs
subsequent calls in the page.route handler and update it so the same logic is
applied to the other similar handler instance mentioned at lines 86-90).
```

</details>

